10101561,2024-09-12,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Account status incorrect,"I am writing to formally dispute the inaccurate information being reported on my credit file. Specifically, [ Account Number ] is being reported as a redeemed repossession on my credit report. This is incorrect and misrepresents the true status of the account. 

1. Violation of the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) 15 U.S.C. 1681e ( b ), credit reporting agencies are required to follow reasonable procedures to ensure maximum possible accuracy of the information they report. Reporting an account as a redeemed repossession when that is factually inaccurate is a clear violation of this obligation. I have not redeemed any repossessed property or vehicle, and the inclusion of this remark is erroneous. 

Furthermore, under 15 U.S.C. 1681i ( a ), I have the right to dispute incomplete or inaccurate information on my credit report. Once notified of a dispute, the credit reporting agency is required to investigate the accuracy of the disputed information within a reasonable time. I am now exercising this right and requesting that you investigate this claim immediately.

2. Violation of the Uniform Commercial Code ( UCC ) 9-625 According to UCC 9-625 , creditors are required to adhere to strict guidelines concerning the repossession and redemption of property. Reporting a repossession inaccurately, when no repossession has legally occurred, violates the principles set forth in the UCC regarding fair and accurate reporting of financial transactions. Moreover, UCC 9-609 details the specific rights of creditors when taking possession of collateral after a default, and no such action has taken place in my case, nor has any redemption.

3. Impact on My Credit Score and Financial Health The inaccurate reporting of a redeemed repossession is unjustly harming my credit score and financial standing. A repossession or the suggestion of one, redeemed or otherwise, implies negative credit behavior, which is not the case here. Reporting such false information is damaging to my ability to access credit, and it violates my rights under the FCRA and UCC.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,"TRANSUNION INTERMEDIATE HOLDINGS, INC.",FL,32506,,Web,2024-09-12,Closed with explanation,Yes
