Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) ( 15 U.S.C. 1681 ), consumers have several key rights to ensure the accuracy, fairness, and privacy of their credit information. Specifically, this law mandates that : Credit reporting agencies must investigate disputes regarding inaccurate or incomplete information on a consumers credit report. They are typically required to resolve such disputes within 30 days. 
Inaccurate, incomplete, or unverifiable information must be corrected or deleted from the consumers credit report. If the information can not be verified within the legally required timeframe, it must be removed. 
Consumers have the right to know what is in their file and can request a free credit report once every 12 months from each of the major credit bureaus. 
Consumers must be informed if information in their credit file is used against them in applications for credit, employment, or insurance. 
Consumers have the right to take legal action against credit reporting agencies or creditors that violate the FCRA, including seeking damages for violations. 

In my case, TransUnion has failed to properly investigate and resolve multiple disputes regarding unauthorized accounts and inquiries on my credit report. Despite providing all required documentation and initiating disputes, these inaccuracies have not been corrected, in violation of FCRA ( 15 U.S.C. 1681 ). Therefore, I am now pursuing legal action to protect my rights and restore the accuracy of my credit report.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,"TRANSUNION INTERMEDIATE HOLDINGS, INC.",FL,34119,,Web,2024-09-26,Closed with non-monetary relief,Yes
